# XView (OPEN LOOK) — Athena-style import for motif-on-qt

This directory is the place for an Athena-style import of the XView toolkit
sources so they can be compiled against motif-on-qt’s Xlib-on-Qt layer.

## Status (transition)

- **Goal**: run unmodified (or minimally adapted) XView library sources on our
  Xlib, then progressively replace packages with thin Qt shims
  (see `docs/xview-xforms-shims.md`).
- **Current**: scaffolding only. XView is substantially larger than XForms;
  vendor the sources here when ready.

## Recommended source trees

XView 3.2p1.4 and derivatives (permissive / original Sun terms + modern ports):

- https://github.com/ElCoyote27/XView  
  (maintained for modern RHEL/Fedora; contains libxview + libolgx)
- https://github.com/ggodd/xview-64bit  
  (64-bit port based on the classic 3.2p1.4 sources)
- Classic SourceForge / OpenWindows trees also exist.

Typical layout after unpacking:

```
lib/libxview/     # core packages (panel, menu, canvas, frame, notify, …)
lib/libolgx/      # OPEN LOOK graphics primitives
```

Copy the library sources into this directory (or into `src/xview/libxview` and
`src/xview/libolgx`) and keep a local `config` / Imake-to-CMake translation.

## Build notes (future)

- XView is **not** Xt-based; it talks Xlib directly and has its own Notifier.
- Include the project’s `include/` X11 headers and link the motif-on-qt archive.
- Missing Xlib surface → fix in `src/xlib.cpp`, do not patch XView sources.
- Notifier must be bridged to the Qt event loop (timeouts, fds, X events).
- libolgx can start as the real sources (drawing through our Xlib) and later
  gain a Qt-style face option analogous to `xaw_qt.cpp`.

## License

Respect the license files that ship with the chosen source tree. Most modern
ports document the original Sun / OpenWindows terms plus any additional
patches.

## Next steps after sources are present

1. CMake option `MQ_BUILD_XVIEW` and static library target(s).
2. Minimal Notifier ↔ Qt event-loop bridge.
3. Smoke-test a simple FRAME + PANEL client under the existing
   `MOTIFQT_SCRIPT` / offscreen harness.
4. Grow the set of packages that have thin Qt shims.
